select energy_manager.load_cards($cards$[
{"id":"p3-b08-027","paper":3,"topic":"Energy Saving Options","kind":"fact","front":"What energy saving have industries reported from reducing lighting feeder voltage with reactors or transformers?","back":"5% to 15% of lighting energy. Industries with high night-time voltage also get fewer premature lamp failures.","explain":"Night-time grid voltages are often higher than normal. Lowering the voltage saves energy and still gives the rated light output, because gas discharge lamps change their light output and power with voltage.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 08 p275","order":3410},
{"id":"p3-b08-028","paper":3,"topic":"Energy Saving Options","kind":"fact","front":"Which electronic ballast advantages does the chapter give over an electromagnetic choke for tube lights?","back":"Electronic ballast losses are about 1 W, against 10 to 15 W for an electromagnetic choke, saving about 15 to 20 W per tube light. The starter is eliminated and the tube lights instantly without flicker.","explain":"Electronic ballasts are oscillators that convert the supply to about 20,000 to 30,000 Hz. They ignite the lamp, stabilise the gas discharge and supply power to the lamp.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 08 p277","order":3411},
{"id":"p3-b08-029","paper":3,"topic":"Energy Saving Options","kind":"fact","front":"How much can ageing lamps and dirt cut illumination, and why is maintenance important?","back":"Together they can reduce total illumination by 50 percent or more, while the lights keep drawing full power. Basic maintenance is cleaning lamps and fixtures, cleaning and repainting interiors, and relamping.","explain":"Lighting maintenance is vital to lighting efficiency. Cleaning and relamping restore the light level without adding any connected load.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 08 p277","order":3412},
{"id":"p3-b08-030","paper":3,"topic":"Lighting Controls","kind":"fact","front":"Which sensing technologies are used for occupancy-linked lighting control?","back":"Infra-red, acoustic, ultrasonic or microwave sensors, which detect either movement or noise in a room.","explain":"The sensors switch lights on when occupancy is detected and off after a set period with no movement. They are designed to override manual switches, so lights are not left on in empty spaces.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 08 p277","order":3413},
{"id":"p3-b08-031","paper":3,"topic":"Lighting Controls","kind":"fact","front":"What is the trade-off in choosing the time span of a timed-turnoff switch?","back":"Shorter time spans waste less energy, but they raise the chance that the lights switch off while someone is still in the space. For most applications the best choice is an electronic unit with a fixed interval set by engineering staff.","explain":"Timed-turnoff switches are the least expensive type of automatic lighting control. Spring-wound dial timers let occupants set the span, which is unlikely to be set with efficiency in mind.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 08 p278","order":3414},
{"id":"p3-b08-032","paper":3,"topic":"Lighting Controls","kind":"fact","front":"Why is an internally mounted photoelectric dimming control better than simple on/off daylight switching?","back":"It senses the total light in the area and adjusts the electric lighting so that daylight plus electric light always meets the design level. Electric light can be turned off completely when daylight alone is enough. Dimming saves more energy and is more acceptable to occupants.","explain":"Time delays are needed in the control system so that fast-moving clouds do not cause repeated rapid switching.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 08 p278","order":3415},
{"id":"p3-b08-033","paper":3,"topic":"Street Lighting","kind":"fact","front":"How many street lights does a typical municipal area have, and what is the typical municipal lighting load?","back":"20,000 to 50,000 street lights, depending on the kilometres of road lit within municipal limits. The typical electrical load of a municipal lighting system is 2 MW to 7 MW.","explain":"Street lighting is one of the major electrical loads in municipal areas. Lamps in use include fluorescent tubes, mercury vapour, sodium vapour and metal halide, with high mast towers at strategic junctions and LEDs for traffic signals.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 08 p278","order":3416},
{"id":"p3-b08-034","paper":3,"topic":"Street Lighting","kind":"fact","front":"Name five controls the chapter lists to reduce street lighting energy use.","back":"Timer control (switch on and off as per set timing); daylight control based on illumination level; selective or alternate switching of low-traffic areas after midnight; lux-level switching after midnight; voltage controllers operated after midnight; and PLC-controlled lighting panels for control and monitoring.","explain":"Several of these controls work only after midnight, when traffic is low. PLC-controlled panels add effective monitoring on top of the switching.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 08 p278","order":3417},
{"id":"p3-b08-035","paper":3,"topic":"Standards and Labelling","kind":"fact","front":"Which fluorescent lamps does the BEE Standard and Labelling programme cover, and up to what wattage?","back":"4-foot tubular fluorescent lamps (FTL) up to 40 W. Halo-phosphate lamps are covered at 6500 K. Tri-phosphate lamps are covered at 6500 K, 4000 K and 2700 K.","explain":"The S&L programme covers FTL because of the large number of these lamps in use. The star rating scheme for FTL is set out in Table 8.4, though the scheme's columns are hard to read in the source text.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 08 p279","order":3418},
{"id":"p3-b08-036","paper":3,"topic":"Lighting Basics","kind":"problem","front":"The illuminance is 10 lm/m² from a lamp at 1 m. What is the illuminance at 0.5 m?","back":"40 lm/m² (40 lx).","explain":"This is the chapter's worked example of the inverse square law. Halving the distance multiplies illuminance by four.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 08 p259","steps":[{"prompt":"Write the inverse square law in the chapter's alternate form.","answer":"E₂ = E₁ × (d₁ / d₂)²","explain":"Illuminance changes with the square of the distance ratio."},{"prompt":"Substitute the values E₁ = 10 lm/m², d₁ = 1.0 m and d₂ = 0.5 m.","answer":"E₂ = 10 × (1.0 / 0.5)²","explain":"Keep the distances in the same unit."},{"prompt":"Evaluate the distance ratio.","answer":"(1.0 / 0.5)² = 2² = 4","explain":"Squaring the ratio is what makes the effect non-linear."},{"prompt":"Multiply to get the new illuminance.","answer":"E₂ = 4 × 10 = 40 lm/m² (40 lx)","explain":"Halving the distance quadruples the illuminance."}],"order":3419},
{"id":"p3-b08-037","paper":3,"topic":"Lighting Basics","kind":"problem","front":"The illuminance is 17 lm/m² at 2 m from a lamp. What is the illuminance at 1.5 m?","back":"About 30.2 lx (17 × (2 / 1.5)² = 30.2 lm/m²).","explain":"This is an exam-style question built directly on the inverse square law. Moving closer to the source increases illuminance by the square of the distance ratio.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 08 p282","steps":[{"prompt":"Write the inverse square relation between the two positions.","answer":"E₂ = E₁ × (d₁ / d₂)²","explain":"Same law as the chapter's worked example."},{"prompt":"Substitute E₁ = 17 lm/m², d₁ = 2 m and d₂ = 1.5 m.","answer":"E₂ = 17 × (2 / 1.5)²","explain":"Check that the distances are in the same unit."},{"prompt":"Evaluate the squared ratio.","answer":"(2 / 1.5)² = (1.333)² = 1.778","explain":"The closer position gives a ratio above 1."},{"prompt":"Multiply to get the answer.","answer":"E₂ = 17 × 1.778 = 30.2 lx","explain":"Illuminance is higher at the closer distance, as expected."}],"order":3420},
{"id":"p3-b08-038","paper":3,"topic":"Illuminance Calculation","kind":"problem","front":"A 10 m × 10 m air-conditioned office needs 200 lux on the working plane. The fittings are twin-tube 36 W lamps with 3050 lm per lamp, UF 0.66 and LLF 0.8. How many fittings are needed? The luminaire is 2.9 m high and the work plane is 0.9 m high.","back":"6 twin-tube fittings (12 lamps). The formula gives 200 × 100 / (2 × 3050 × 0.66 × 0.8) = 6.2, which the book rounds to 6.","explain":"This is the chapter's worked example of the zonal cavity method. The book rounds 6.2 down to 6. Strictly, 6 fittings give about 193 lux, so exam answers should follow the book's 6.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 08 p272","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the room index.","answer":"Mounting height = 2.9 − 0.9 = 2.0 m; RI = (10 × 10) / [2.0 × (10 + 10)] = 100 / 40 = 2.5","explain":"The room index links the room geometry to the utilisation factor table."},{"prompt":"Read the utilisation factor.","answer":"UF = 0.66 for the twin-tube fitting at room index 2.5","explain":"The UF comes from the manufacturer's table for this luminaire."},{"prompt":"Pick the light loss factor.","answer":"LLF = 0.8 for an air-conditioned office","explain":"Typical values are 0.8 air-conditioned office, 0.7 clean industrial and 0.6 dirty industrial."},{"prompt":"Compute the total lumens from all lamps in one fitting.","answer":"F = 2 lamps × 3050 lm = 6100 lm","explain":"A twin-tube fitting carries two lamps."},{"prompt":"Apply N = (E × A) / (F × UF × LLF).","answer":"N = (200 × 100) / (6100 × 0.66 × 0.8) = 20000 / 3220.8 = 6.2","explain":"E is 200 lux and A is 100 m²."},{"prompt":"Round to whole fittings.","answer":"6 fittings, i.e. 12 × 36 W lamps","explain":"The book's answer is 6 fittings."}],"order":3421},
{"id":"p3-b08-039","paper":3,"topic":"Illuminance Calculation","kind":"problem","front":"Luminaires are spaced 3.33 m apart and mounted 2.0 m above the work plane. What is the space-to-height ratio, and is it acceptable against the recommended value of 1.5?","back":"SHR = 3.33 / 2.0 = 1.66. It is slightly above the recommended 1.5, and the book accepts it as close to the limit.","explain":"Because SHR is above 1.5, the uniformity of lighting is slightly less than ideal. The book notes that a larger SHR is better, since it can reduce the number of fittings and the connected load.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 08 p273","steps":[{"prompt":"Take the luminaire spacing.","answer":"Spacing = 10 / 3 = 3.33 m","explain":"This is the spacing worked out in the chapter's example."},{"prompt":"Find the mounting height.","answer":"Mounting height = 2.9 − 0.9 = 2.0 m","explain":"Mounting height is luminaire height minus work plane height."},{"prompt":"Compute the space-to-height ratio.","answer":"SHR = 3.33 / 2.0 = 1.66","explain":"SHR is spacing divided by mounting height."},{"prompt":"Compare against the recommended value.","answer":"1.66 is above 1.5, so it is close to the limit and accepted in the chapter","explain":"Values above the recommendation slightly reduce uniformity."}],"order":3422},
{"id":"p3-b08-040","paper":3,"topic":"Lighting Case Study","kind":"problem","front":"Forty T12 fluorescent fixtures (40 W lamp plus 12 W ballast) run 12 hours a day, every day of the year. What is the connected load and the annual energy use?","back":"About 2.1 kW connected load and about 25 kWh per day, or 9,125 kWh per year.","explain":"This is the existing case in the chapter's lighting case study on campus security lighting. The book rounds 2.08 kW to 2.1 kW.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 08 p279","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the power of one fixture.","answer":"40 W + 12 W ballast = 52 W","explain":"Ballast losses are part of the fixture's real draw."},{"prompt":"Find the connected load for 40 fixtures.","answer":"40 × 52 W = 2080 W = 2.08 kW (book: 2.1 kW)","explain":"Connected load is the total of all fixtures running together."},{"prompt":"Find the daily energy.","answer":"2.08 kW × 12 h = 24.96 ≈ 25 kWh/day","explain":"Hours of operation times the load gives the daily energy."},{"prompt":"Find the annual energy.","answer":"25 kWh/day × 365 = 9,125 kWh/year","explain":"Matches the book's annual figure."}],"order":3423},
{"id":"p3-b08-041","paper":3,"topic":"Lighting Case Study","kind":"problem","front":"The 40 T12 fixtures are replaced with 18 W LEDs that draw 0.72 kW for 12 hours a day. At Rs 5 per unit and Rs 2,500 per lamp, what is the annual saving and the simple payback?","back":"Saving of 5,840 kWh per year, worth Rs 29,200 per year. The investment is Rs 1.0 lakh, so the simple payback is about 3.4 years (book figures).","explain":"The book gives 3,285 kWh per year for the LED case. The arithmetic 0.72 kW × 12 h × 365 days gives about 3,154 kWh, so the book's figure is slightly higher. Exam answers should follow the book's numbers.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 08 p280","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the LED connected load.","answer":"40 × 18 W = 720 W = 0.72 kW","explain":"Replacing the lamps removes the ballast losses as well."},{"prompt":"Find the proposed annual energy (book value).","answer":"3,285 kWh/year (book figure; 0.72 × 12 × 365 would give about 3,154)","explain":"Note the small gap between the book figure and the simple calculation."},{"prompt":"Find the annual energy saving.","answer":"9,125 − 3,285 = 5,840 kWh/year","explain":"Saving is the existing energy minus the proposed energy."},{"prompt":"Convert the saving to money.","answer":"5,840 kWh × Rs 5 = Rs 29,200 per year","explain":"Uses the tariff given in the book."},{"prompt":"Find the investment.","answer":"40 lamps × Rs 2,500 = Rs 1,00,000 (Rs 1.0 lakh)","explain":"Investment is the lamp cost for all 40 lamps."},{"prompt":"Find the simple payback.","answer":"1,00,000 / 29,200 = 3.4 years","explain":"Simple payback is investment divided by annual money saving."}],"order":3424},
{"id":"p3-b08-042","paper":3,"topic":"Lighting Case Study","kind":"problem","front":"An 18 W LED with an efficacy of 130 lm/W gives how many lumens?","back":"2,340 lumens (18 × 130), matching the book's proposed LED output.","explain":"The chapter's proposal gives an efficacy of about 120 to 140 lm/W, which gives 2,160 to 2,520 lumens for an 18 W lamp. The book's figure of 2,340 lumens corresponds to 130 lm/W.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 08 p279","steps":[{"prompt":"Write the lumen formula.","answer":"Lumens = efficacy (lm/W) × power (W)","explain":"Efficacy is lumens per watt, so multiplying by watts gives lumens."},{"prompt":"Substitute efficacy 130 lm/W and power 18 W.","answer":"Lumens = 130 × 18","explain":"Use the efficacy mid-point of the chapter's 120 to 140 lm/W range."},{"prompt":"Compute the result.","answer":"2,340 lumens","explain":"This is the book's total output for the replacement lamps."},{"prompt":"Check against the range.","answer":"120 to 140 lm/W gives 2,160 to 2,520 lumens","explain":"The book's figure sits inside this range."}],"order":3425},
{"id":"p3-b08-043","paper":3,"topic":"Lamp Types","kind":"problem","front":"A 250 W HPSV lamp is to be replaced by two 28 W CFLs running 3,000 hours a year. What is the annual energy saving, and should the change be recommended?","back":"Saving of 582 kWh per year (750 − 168). The watts alone do not settle it: check that the light output and colour meet the task before recommending the change.","explain":"The chapter gives this as an exam question without a model answer. The chapter stresses matching the lamp type to the work and keeping adequate light levels, so the output check is the key point.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 08 p282","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the HPSV annual energy.","answer":"250 W × 3,000 h = 750 kWh/year","explain":"Energy is power times hours."},{"prompt":"Find the CFL annual energy.","answer":"2 × 28 W × 3,000 h = 168 kWh/year","explain":"Two CFLs replace one HPSV lamp."},{"prompt":"Find the annual saving.","answer":"750 − 168 = 582 kWh/year","explain":"Multiply by the tariff to get the money saving, which the question does not give."},{"prompt":"Decide whether to recommend the change.","answer":"Recommend only if the light output and colour rendering meet the task; check the lumen output of the CFLs against the HPSV","explain":"The chapter says to establish adequate light levels without compromising productivity, safety or security."}],"order":3426},
{"id":"p3-b08-044","paper":3,"topic":"Lighting Case Study","kind":"problem","front":"A municipal corporation has 30,000 lamps of 250 W HPMV each, run 3,600 hours a year, at a tariff of Rs 4.75 per kWh. What is the annual energy consumption and the annual energy cost?","back":"27,000,000 kWh per year, costing about Rs 12.825 crore (Rs 128.25 million) per year.","explain":"This is the energy part of exam question L-2. The same question asks for demand cost at Rs 150 per kVA with 0.7 PF, but the chapter does not state the billing period, so that part is not carded.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 08 p282","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the total connected load.","answer":"30,000 × 250 W = 7,500 kW","explain":"Total load is the number of lamps times the wattage per lamp."},{"prompt":"Find the annual energy.","answer":"7,500 kW × 3,600 h = 27,000,000 kWh/year","explain":"Energy is load times annual operating hours."},{"prompt":"Find the annual energy cost.","answer":"27,000,000 kWh × Rs 4.75 = Rs 1,28,250,000 (Rs 12.825 crore)","explain":"Multiply energy by the tariff per kWh."}],"order":3427},
{"id":"p3-b09-001","paper":3,"topic":"DG Set Systems","kind":"fact","front":"What are the two basic types of reciprocating engines used to generate electricity?","back":"Spark ignition (SI) engines and compression ignition (CI) engines.","explain":"Spark ignition engines use a spark plug to ignite a compressed fuel-air mixture. Compression ignition engines compress air until it is hot enough to ignite injected fuel, and their higher compression ratio gives them higher efficiency.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 09 p285","order":3428},
{"id":"p3-b09-002","paper":3,"topic":"DG Set Systems","kind":"fact","front":"Which fuels are typically used in spark ignition engines?","back":"Gasoline, natural gas, and sewage and landfill gas.","explain":"Spark ignition engines suit gaseous and light fuels that are mixed with air before combustion. Diesel and heavy fuel oil are normally used in compression ignition engines.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 09 p285","order":3429},
{"id":"p3-b09-003","paper":3,"topic":"DG Set Systems","kind":"fact","front":"Which fuel is normally used in compression ignition engines, and how does a dual-fuel engine work?","back":"Diesel or heavy fuel oil is normally used. In a dual-fuel engine, natural gas is compressed with the combustion air and diesel oil is injected at the top of the compression stroke to start combustion.","explain":"The diesel oil acts as the ignition source for the gas-air mixture. The engine can therefore run mainly on natural gas while still using diesel to initiate combustion.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 09 p285","order":3430},
{"id":"p3-b09-004","paper":3,"topic":"DG Set Systems","kind":"fact","front":"What compression ratio range is typical for a diesel engine, and what temperature does the compressed air reach?","back":"14:1 to 25:1. The air is heated to about 700-900°C, which ignites the injected diesel spontaneously.","explain":"Because the fuel ignites from compression heat, the diesel engine is also called a compression ignition (CI) engine. The high compression ratio is why CI engines are more efficient than spark ignition engines.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 09 p286","order":3431},
{"id":"p3-b09-005","paper":3,"topic":"DG Set Systems","kind":"fact","front":"Name the four strokes of a four-stroke diesel engine in order.","back":"Induction, compression, ignition and power, exhaust.","explain":"On induction the descending piston draws in fresh air. During compression the valves are closed and the air is compressed to up to 25 bar. Fuel is injected and ignites on the power stroke, and the exhaust stroke discharges the spent gases.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 09 p286","order":3432},
{"id":"p3-b09-006","paper":3,"topic":"DG Set Systems","kind":"fact","front":"What is the approximate electrical efficiency of a modern lean-burn spark ignition gas engine?","back":"Close to 45%.","explain":"In a lean-burn gas engine, gas is mixed with air before the inlet valves, and a small prechamber with a richer mixture is ignited by a spark plug. Flames from the prechamber nozzle ignite the lean mixture in the main cylinder, so combustion is fast.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 09 p287","order":3433},
{"id":"p3-b09-007","paper":3,"topic":"DG Set Systems","kind":"fact","front":"List the five components that make a diesel generating set a system.","back":"(a) Diesel engine and accessories, (b) AC generator, (c) control systems and switchgear, (d) foundation and power house civil works, (e) the connected load and its components such as heating, motor drives and lighting.","explain":"The set performs well only when these components are well matched. The chapter asks that each component be chosen for highest efficiency and run near its optimum efficiency level.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 09 p287","order":3434},
{"id":"p3-b09-008","paper":3,"topic":"DG Set Systems","kind":"fact","front":"By how much should the engine power rating exceed the end-use power demand?","back":"By 10-20%.","explain":"The margin stops the machine being overloaded when motors start, when some lighting switches on, or when wear raises power consumption. The engine should not be sized exactly at the demand.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 09 p288","order":3435},
{"id":"p3-b09-009","paper":3,"topic":"DG Set Systems","kind":"fact","front":"What speed range do diesel engines typically run at, and where should an engine be run?","back":"Typically 1300-3000 RPM. Run the engine as close as possible to its rated speed.","explain":"Running at rated speed avoids poor efficiency and deposit build-up from incomplete combustion, which raises maintenance and running costs. Speed is measured at the output shaft, and fuel efficiency is greatest at one optimum speed.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 09 p288","order":3436},
{"id":"p3-b09-010","paper":3,"topic":"DG Set Systems","kind":"fact","front":"What captive power output range do diesel engine plants most frequently cover?","back":"Most frequently used sizes are 4 to 15 MW.","explain":"Diesel plants dominate small captive (non-utility) power because diesel engines are more efficient than gas turbines and small steam turbines in this range. For continuous operation a low speed engine is more cost-effective than a high speed engine.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 09 p288","order":3437},
{"id":"p3-b09-011","paper":3,"topic":"DG Set Systems","kind":"fact","front":"Give four advantages of a captive diesel power plant.","back":"Any four of: low installation cost; short delivery and installation period; efficiency as high as 43-45%; better performance at part loads; minimum cooling water needs; short start-up time.","explain":"Diesel plants also suit a range of fuels, from light distillates to residual fuel oils. They can use air cooled heat exchangers where water is scarce.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 09 p289","order":3438},
{"id":"p3-b09-012","paper":3,"topic":"DG Set Systems","kind":"fact","front":"How has the specific fuel consumption of diesel engines changed since the 1970s?","back":"It has fallen from about 220 g/kWh in the 1970s to about 160 g/kWh at present.","explain":"Modern high efficiency turbochargers allow an exhaust gas driven turbine generator to add output. This lowers fuel consumption per kWh and raises overall thermal efficiency.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 09 p289","order":3439},
{"id":"p3-b09-013","paper":3,"topic":"DG Set Systems","kind":"fact","front":"Can a gas turbine burn the poorest quality fuel oils as easily as a diesel engine can?","back":"No. A diesel engine can burn the poorest quality fuel oils, while a gas turbine needs costly fuel treatment equipment to do so.","explain":"This is a common exam trap. Slow speed dual fuel engines using high-pressure gas injection also give the same thermal efficiency and power output as a regular fuel oil engine.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 09 p289","order":3440},
{"id":"p3-b09-014","paper":3,"topic":"DG Set Systems","kind":"fact","front":"What is the normally accepted definition of a high speed engine?","back":"An engine running at 1500 rpm.","explain":"High speed sets have been developed in India, while slow speed engines of higher capacity are often imported. Slow speed engines suit continuous use, and high speed engines suit intermittent use.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 09 p290","order":3441},
{"id":"p3-b09-015","paper":3,"topic":"DG Set Systems","kind":"fact","front":"Which engine type has the longer period between overhauls, and what are the typical intervals?","back":"The slow speed engine, at about 8000 hours. The high speed engine is about 3200 hours (typical manufacturer recommendations).","explain":"Slow speed engines have lower BMEP, less wear and tear, a higher weight-to-power ratio and lower direct operating cost. These features make them suitable for continuous use.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 09 p290","order":3442},
{"id":"p3-b09-016","paper":3,"topic":"DG Set Systems","kind":"problem","front":"A plant has a 650 kW connected load, a diversity factor of 0.54, a 70% set loading and 0.8 PF. What DG set rating in kVA is needed?","back":"About 625 kVA (maximum demand 350 kW, set rating 500 kW).","explain":"Diversity factor here is maximum demand divided by connected load. The set should run at about 70% loading, and the kW rating is converted to kVA using the power factor.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 09 p290","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the maximum demand from the connected load and diversity factor.","answer":"Maximum demand = 650 kW × 0.54 = 351 kW, taken as 350 kW.","explain":"The diversity factor is demand divided by connected load, so demand = connected load × factor."},{"prompt":"Find the set rating in kW for 70% loading.","answer":"Set rating = 350 kW / 0.70 = 500 kW.","explain":"The set is sized so that the demand is about 70% of its rating."},{"prompt":"Convert the kW rating to kVA at 0.8 PF.","answer":"kVA = 500 / 0.8 = 625 kVA.","explain":"Generators are specified in kVA, so the kW rating is divided by the power factor."}],"order":3443},
{"id":"p3-b09-017","paper":3,"topic":"DG Set Systems","kind":"fact","front":"Why is one large DG set usually preferred over two or more smaller sets?","back":"One large set is more economical in space, operation, maintenance and initial capital investment.","explain":"Parallel sets have their own benefits. Only the power shortfall needs to be met, one set can be stopped while another carries at least 50% of the requirement, and one set can serve as 100% standby in low power-cut periods.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 09 p291","order":3444},
{"id":"p3-b09-018","paper":3,"topic":"DG Set Systems","kind":"fact","front":"Which protections are recommended on all DG sets, and which relay is essential for parallel operation?","back":"Short circuit, overload and earth fault protection, plus high temperature and low lube oil pressure cut-outs. A reverse power relay is essential when sets run in parallel.","explain":"Full circuit protection may be uneconomical on small sets, but it is strongly recommended. The reverse power relay prevents one alternator from back feeding another.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 09 p291","order":3445},
{"id":"p3-b09-019","paper":3,"topic":"DG Set Systems","kind":"fact","front":"What is the largest direct-on-line (DOL) motor a DG set can start, and what starting method raises that limit?","back":"Roughly 50% of the DG set kVA rating in HP with DOL starting. Star-delta or auto-transformer starting raises the limit to about 75% of the kVA rating.","explain":"DOL starting draws up to six times rated current for a few seconds, and the starting current should not exceed about 200% of alternator full load capacity. Voltage and frequency recover well before the motor reaches full speed.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 09 p292","order":3446},
{"id":"p3-b09-020","paper":3,"topic":"DG Set Systems","kind":"fact","front":"What does the electricity rules require for earthing a DG set?","back":"Two independent earths, one to the equipment body and one to the neutral.","explain":"Two independent earths give adequate protection against earth faults. They also drain away any leakage potential from the equipment for safe working.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 09 p292","order":3447},
{"id":"p3-b09-021","paper":3,"topic":"DG Set Systems","kind":"problem","front":"A 500 kVA DG set is to start the largest possible motor. What is the limit in HP with DOL starting, and with star-delta or auto-transformer starting?","back":"About 250 HP with DOL starting, and about 375 HP with star-delta or auto-transformer starting.","explain":"The chapter gives these as approximate guides for the largest motor the set can start. Reduced-voltage starting cuts the starting current, which allows a larger motor.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 09 p292","steps":[{"prompt":"State the rule for the largest motor started by DOL.","answer":"Largest DOL motor (HP) is about 50% of the DG set kVA rating.","explain":"This follows from the limit on starting current for the alternator."},{"prompt":"Apply the DOL rule to a 500 kVA set.","answer":"0.50 × 500 = 250 HP.","explain":"The result is an approximate limit, not an exact value."},{"prompt":"Apply the star-delta or auto-transformer rule.","answer":"0.75 × 500 = 375 HP.","explain":"Reduced-voltage starting lowers the starting current, so the largest motor can be about 75% of the kVA rating."}],"order":3448}
]$cards$::jsonb);
